> process is placed in /usr/sbin, owned by root:system.
>     -- What should the right way to do so?
>     -- Which file should keep those "export" statement?
> 
> A simple script with export statement, named "S999TEST" are placed in
> /sbin/rc2.d and /sbin/rc3.d. But, after system is rebooted,  still not make
> any affect.
> 
> S999TEST:
> --------------------------------------------------
> TEST_ENV_003=AlbertoErica003
> export TEST_ENV_003
> --------------------------------------------------

ENVIRONMENT variables are purely a shell concept.  Each account that 
actually has a shell specified in it's passwd entry and creates 
processes would need any global environment variables in it's .profile 
(sh) or .cshrc (csh) etc such that any process they create picks up 
these environment variables when a new shell is spawned.

Having said this there are very few reasons why you should want to place 
environment variables in such a global position.  It is better practice 
to do as the other post said and create a startup script for your daemon 
and place any environment variables within it.

Even better again would be to develop your daemon further and add the 
ability for it to accept arguments on the command line to specify any 
info it might need.



