The thing that I find really strange about the whole "C2 security" package
concept, is that most of the things that get put in such a package are not
actually required for C2 security. The only thing that your average stock
UNIX box (at any time in the past 20 years !) doesn't support that C2
requires is the file access auditing.

All the rest, per-user ACLs, the supposedly root-proof TCB, even the shadow
password file, they're not actually part of the orange book requirements.
The UNIX user/group/other ACLs and encrypted passwords alongside reasonable
account management policies (which you need in any case) are quite enough
to satisfy the letter and the spirit of the book.

> However, I still have many problems. If I only want to activate the
> Shadow function on Tru64, do you think it is a must to enable C2
> security on my Tru64 server?  Is that free of charge as default? 
> Thanks.  ;)

We looked into this a few years back. You can't use the C2 package to get
shadow passwords without dragging the rest of the stuff in.

You're better off installing one of the open source shadow password packages
instead. Ideal would be one that worked as an SIA plugin, but the only SIA
plugin I've found is a Kerberos5 one.

ftp://ftp.pld.org.pl/software/shadow/shadow-4.0.3.tar.gz

This has hooks to work as a PAM module. It may be possible to hook it into
SIA, with something like:

siad_ses_authent=(BSD,/local/lib/libshadow.so)

and so on, for the other authentication and password change hooks.

