> Thanks all to share lots of experience to me. May I summarize my
> question once again?  If I really need to shadow the password,
> 
> * Method 1: to launch the 'secconfig' utility configure the custom as
> enhanced security.
> * Method 2: to create accounts in /var/tcb/files/auth.db file
> 
> Am I right?


When you run secconfig - all the accounts in /etc/passwd (or anything 
obtainable through getpwent() will be "migrated" into the auth.db.  Each 
account is still listed in /etc/passwd, but the passwd field is the 
replaced with an asterisk ("*").

I think this answers the "issue" below...


Once you are running enhanced security, the provided sysadmin tools 
(adduser, dxaccounts, etc.) will add users to both /etc/passwd and 
auth.db automagically.  If though you have your own homebrewed new user 
tool, then once it adds the account to /etc/passwd (in whatever manner 
you choose to do so), just run the 'convuser' utility and the account 
will also be added to the auth.db file.  Check the convuser man page for 
the exact switches you would want.


