ksh: ulimit: exceeds allowable limit

You may well be right about previous versions of Tru64 not permitting ulimit 
raising (I can't get it to work now, although I could have *sworn* it used to), 
but that still begs the question: WHO is deciding that 4M is enough stack space 
and setting the HARD limit to that?  The whole point of having ulimit is to 
allow users to customise this stuff.  For example, on another system:

bond $ cat /etc/motd
Sun Microsystems Inc.   SunOS 5.8       Generic February 2000
bond $ ulimit -s
8192
bond $ ulimit -Hs
unlimited
bond $ ulimit -s 20480
bond $ ulimit -s
20480

The reason I said "root can set it to 20M" is just to point out that root *can* 
increase its limit (presumably up to 32M) so we are not running into some 
absolute system defined limit here.  I can make it work by having root set the 
limit and getting it "inherited":

austin $ id
uid=0(root) gid=1(daemon)groups=0(system),3(mem),7(terminal),9(opr),12(lp)
austin $ ulimit -s
20480
austin $ su aUser
austin $ id
uid=200(aUser) gid=15(users)
austin $ ulimit -s
20480

... but this is clearly very cumbersome.  I will try some more experiments to 
see if e.g. I can get login to start with a higher limit and pass it along to 
the login shell.

I have looked through the documentation I can find until I'm sick to death of 
it, and all it mentions is the kernel subsystem settings and use of ulimit.  So 
either I am missing something subtle, or there is more to it than they are 
revealing.

Thanks anyway.  If you have any more insights I'd like to hear them.



