I found my problem - finally.  Thanks to all who responded.  It turned out to be 
a combination of carelessness (on my part) and subtlety (on Tru64's).

man ulimit spells it out, if I read it carefully:

[Tru64 UNIX]   If neither the -H option nor the -S option is specified, the
   limit applies to both hard and soft limits.  If only the -a option is
   specified, soft limits are reported.

"... both hard and soft...".  So if I incant:
    ulimit -s 4096
I can now no longer raise my own limit.  On the other hand, if I do:
    ulimit -Ss 4096
the soft limit is 4M and I can raise it up to the pre-existing hard limit any 
time after.

It never occurred to me that the default action was to set the hard limit, so I 
never looked really hard for places where ulimit might have snuck in.  Needless 
to say there was one, to which I have now added the magic '-S' option.

Well, that was a pain I didn't need :-)

